WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.673
Welcome to Week Five of CS104: Sets and Functions.

2
00:00:04.673 --> 00:00:10.904
We now use the language of logic and proof to describe collections

3
00:00:10.904 --> 00:00:14.538
of objects and rules that connect them.

4
00:00:14.538 --> 00:00:18.173
Sets tell us which objects belong together.

5
00:00:18.173 --> 00:00:22.327
Functions assign an output to each permitted input.

6
00:00:22.327 --> 00:00:28.038
Our aim is to move comfortably between notation, diagrams, and proofs.

7
00:00:28.038 --> 00:00:34.269
In particular, we will distinguish membership from containment, a codomain from a

8
00:00:34.269 --> 00:00:40.500
range, and the two separate requirements that make a function a bijection.

9
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.313
The lecture has two parts.

10
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:50.063
We begin with ways to represent sets, then study subsets, proper subsets,

11
00:00:50.063 --> 00:00:54.000
and operations such as union and intersection.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.625
Cartesian products introduce ordered pairs and connect sets with geometry.

13
00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:03.000
The second part defines functions precisely.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.750
We identify domains, codomains, images, preimages, and ranges, before proving whether a

15
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.125
function is injective, surjective, or both.

16
00:01:13.125 --> 00:01:19.875
Each definition provides a proof strategy, so keep asking what an arbitrary

17
00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:25.500
element must satisfy and what would count as a counterexample.

18
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.065
Section five point one begins with sets.

19
00:01:29.065 --> 00:01:35.176
A useful habit is to name the underlying collection before applying a

20
00:01:35.176 --> 00:01:35.685
condition.

21
00:01:35.685 --> 00:01:41.796
For example, integer solutions and real solutions to an equation need not

22
00:01:41.796 --> 00:01:43.833
form the same set.

23
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:49.944
We will make these choices explicit, then use the resulting membership statements

24
00:01:49.944 --> 00:01:53.000
as the starting point for proofs.

25
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.346
A set is a well-defined collection of objects.

26
00:01:57.346 --> 00:02:01.691
Well-defined means that membership has a precise criterion.

27
00:02:01.691 --> 00:02:07.667
The positive integers, rational numbers, and real numbers are familiar examples.

28
00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:14.185
The integer solutions of x squared equals four form the set containing

29
00:02:14.185 --> 00:02:16.358
negative two and two.

30
00:02:16.358 --> 00:02:20.704
Reciprocals of positive integers form another infinite set.

31
00:02:20.704 --> 00:02:25.593
Positive real numbers below ten form a continuous interval.

32
00:02:25.593 --> 00:02:32.111
Notice that the objects need not be listed one by one, but

33
00:02:32.111 --> 00:02:37.000
the description must determine exactly which objects are included.

34
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.413
Roster notation lists the elements between braces.

35
00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:46.263
For positive integers we write one, two, three, four, and so on.

36
00:02:46.263 --> 00:02:52.112
For reciprocals we list one, one half, one third, and so on.

37
00:02:52.112 --> 00:02:57.962
A condition is more suitable for positive real numbers less than ten:

38
00:02:57.962 --> 00:03:03.812
x is real, and zero is less than x, which is less

39
00:03:03.812 --> 00:03:04.787
than ten.

40
00:03:04.787 --> 00:03:06.737
Both endpoints are excluded.

41
00:03:06.737 --> 00:03:10.637
The underlying set and the condition work together.

42
00:03:10.637 --> 00:03:16.000
Changing real numbers to integers would produce a different, finite collection.

43
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.473
Set-builder notation reads: the set of x in U such that p

44
00:03:21.473 --> 00:03:22.841
of x holds.

45
00:03:22.841 --> 00:03:27.857
U supplies the permitted objects, and p supplies the membership test.

46
00:03:27.857 --> 00:03:33.330
For odd integers, the condition is that x equals two k plus

47
00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.610
one for some integer k.

48
00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:41.082
Equivalently, we may collect the expressions two k plus one as k

49
00:03:41.082 --> 00:03:42.907
ranges over all integers.

50
00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:48.379
Negative values of k matter: this is the set of all odd

51
00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:50.659
integers, including negative odd integers.

52
00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:56.132
The vertical bar means such that; it is not a divisibility sign

53
00:03:56.132 --> 00:03:57.500
in this position.

54
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:02.593
A is a subset of B when every element of A is

55
00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:04.715
also an element of B.

56
00:04:04.715 --> 00:04:07.686
The diagram places A entirely inside B.

57
00:04:07.686 --> 00:04:12.779
In logical language, for every x in the underlying universe, membership in

58
00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:14.901
A implies membership in B.

59
00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:19.994
This does not say that every member of B belongs to A.

60
00:04:19.994 --> 00:04:25.087
Also distinguish the symbols: x belongs to A compares an object with

61
00:04:25.087 --> 00:04:30.180
a set, whereas A is a subset of B compares two sets.

62
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.000
A subset is allowed to equal the containing set.

63
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.012
Let A be the integers divisible by six and B the even

64
00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:39.429
integers.

65
00:04:39.429 --> 00:04:44.441
To prove A is a subset of B, take an arbitrary x

66
00:04:44.441 --> 00:04:45.276
in A.

67
00:04:45.276 --> 00:04:49.453
By divisibility, x equals six m for some integer m.

68
00:04:49.453 --> 00:04:52.376
Rewrite this as two times three m.

69
00:04:52.376 --> 00:04:57.388
Since three m is an integer, x is even, so x belongs

70
00:04:57.388 --> 00:04:58.224
to B.

71
00:04:58.224 --> 00:05:03.235
The arbitrary choice is essential: we did not merely check six or

72
00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:03.653
twelve.

73
00:05:03.653 --> 00:05:08.665
We showed that any member of A automatically satisfies the defining condition

74
00:05:08.665 --> 00:05:09.500
for B.

75
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:14.802
Negating a subset claim changes a universal statement into an existential one.

76
00:05:14.802 --> 00:05:20.105
A is not a subset of B means there exists an element

77
00:05:20.105 --> 00:05:23.198
in A that is not in B.

78
00:05:23.198 --> 00:05:28.500
In the diagram, the marked x lies in the part of A

79
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:29.384
outside B.

80
00:05:29.384 --> 00:05:34.244
One such witness is sufficient, but both parts must be checked.

81
00:05:34.244 --> 00:05:39.547
An object outside both sets would not refute the claim, and neither

82
00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:43.081
would an object that lies only in B.

83
00:05:43.081 --> 00:05:47.500
The direction of containment determines where the witness must lie.

84
00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.907
Here A contains integers whose square is divisible by four, while B

85
00:05:52.907 --> 00:05:55.611
contains integers themselves divisible by four.

86
00:05:55.611 --> 00:05:57.864
Choose x equal to two.

87
00:05:57.864 --> 00:06:03.272
Its square is four, so four divides its square and two belongs

88
00:06:03.272 --> 00:06:04.173
to A.

89
00:06:04.173 --> 00:06:09.580
But four does not divide two, so two does not belong to

90
00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:10.031
B.

91
00:06:10.031 --> 00:06:15.438
This completes the proof that A is not a subset of B.

92
00:06:15.438 --> 00:06:20.846
Squaring can introduce an extra factor, so divisibility of a square must

93
00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:24.000
not be transferred unchanged to its base.

94
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.299
Set equality requires both inclusions.

95
00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:31.816
In this example, four divides x squared exactly when x is even.

96
00:06:31.816 --> 00:06:35.954
For the first direction, suppose four divides x squared.

97
00:06:35.954 --> 00:06:41.471
If x were odd, writing x as two k plus one would

98
00:06:41.471 --> 00:06:46.989
make its square four times k squared plus k, plus one, which

99
00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:49.287
cannot be divisible by four.

100
00:06:49.287 --> 00:06:51.126
Thus x is even.

101
00:06:51.126 --> 00:06:56.644
Conversely, if x equals two k, then its square equals four k

102
00:06:56.644 --> 00:06:58.943
squared, so four divides it.

103
00:06:58.943 --> 00:07:04.000
Each direction starts from one membership condition and reaches the other.

104
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.489
Every set is a subset of itself, because each of its elements

105
00:07:09.489 --> 00:07:12.234
is certainly in that same set.

106
00:07:12.234 --> 00:07:16.351
A proper subset additionally requires inequality of the sets.

107
00:07:16.351 --> 00:07:21.840
To prove A is a proper subset of B, prove containment and

108
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.500
then find an element of B outside A.

109
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.989
Multiples of four are a proper subset of the integers whose squares

110
00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:32.819
are divisible by four.

111
00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:38.309
Every multiple of four has such a square, while two belongs to

112
00:07:38.309 --> 00:07:42.883
the larger set but is not a multiple of four.

113
00:07:42.883 --> 00:07:47.000
Ordinary subset notation alone does not assert this difference.

114
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.845
The empty set has no elements, so its cardinality is zero.

115
00:07:51.845 --> 00:07:54.929
It is a subset of every set.

116
00:07:54.929 --> 00:08:00.214
To see why, write the subset condition as: if x belongs to

117
00:08:00.214 --> 00:08:03.738
the empty set, then x belongs to A.

118
00:08:03.738 --> 00:08:07.702
The hypothesis never holds, so the implication cannot fail.

119
00:08:07.702 --> 00:08:11.226
There is no counterexample in the empty set.

120
00:08:11.226 --> 00:08:16.512
Do not confuse the empty set with the set containing the number

121
00:08:16.512 --> 00:08:20.476
zero, or with the set containing the empty set.

122
00:08:20.476 --> 00:08:24.000
Each of those latter sets has one element.

123
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.809
Intersection selects elements in both sets, so it corresponds to logical and.

124
00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:35.617
Union selects elements in at least one set, so its or is

125
00:08:35.617 --> 00:08:36.101
inclusive.

126
00:08:36.101 --> 00:08:41.910
The complement of A selects elements of the chosen universe that are

127
00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:43.362
not in A.

128
00:08:43.362 --> 00:08:49.170
Set difference B minus A selects elements in B but outside A.

129
00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:54.979
The universe is essential for complements, and the order is essential for

130
00:08:54.979 --> 00:08:55.463
difference.

131
00:08:55.463 --> 00:09:01.271
As a quick example, with A containing one and two, and B

132
00:09:01.271 --> 00:09:07.080
containing two and three, their intersection contains only two, while their union

133
00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.500
contains one, two, and three.

134
00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:12.298
Read the numbered Venn regions carefully.

135
00:09:12.298 --> 00:09:15.096
Region one is outside both circles.

136
00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:17.893
Region two is in A only.

137
00:09:17.893 --> 00:09:20.225
Region three is their overlap.

138
00:09:20.225 --> 00:09:23.022
Region four is in B only.

139
00:09:23.022 --> 00:09:28.618
Therefore the intersection is region three, and the union consists of regions

140
00:09:28.618 --> 00:09:30.483
two, three, and four.

141
00:09:30.483 --> 00:09:34.680
The complement of A contains regions one and four.

142
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:40.275
A minus B is region two, while B minus A is region

143
00:09:40.275 --> 00:09:40.742
four.

144
00:09:40.742 --> 00:09:44.472
The intersection of both complements is region one.

145
00:09:44.472 --> 00:09:50.067
Finally, A intersect the complement of B is region two, matching A

146
00:09:50.067 --> 00:09:51.000
minus B.

147
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.188
We prove that A minus B equals A intersect the complement of

148
00:09:56.188 --> 00:09:56.620
B.

149
00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:01.807
If x belongs to A minus B, then x belongs to A

150
00:10:01.807 --> 00:10:04.401
and does not belong to B.

151
00:10:04.401 --> 00:10:09.589
The second condition says x belongs to the complement of B, so

152
00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:12.182
x belongs to the required intersection.

153
00:10:12.182 --> 00:10:16.505
For the reverse inclusion, start with x in that intersection.

154
00:10:16.505 --> 00:10:21.693
It lies in A and in the complement of B, hence in

155
00:10:21.693 --> 00:10:26.880
A but not B, which means x lies in A minus B.

156
00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.609
Both inclusions establish equality.

157
00:10:28.609 --> 00:10:32.500
A diagram suggests the identity; membership reasoning proves it.

158
00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:37.720
The familiar laws of logic reappear as laws of sets.

159
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:43.462
Repeating an intersection or union with the same set changes nothing.

160
00:10:43.462 --> 00:10:47.637
Commutative and associative laws allow reordering and regrouping.

161
00:10:47.637 --> 00:10:50.769
Each operation distributes over the other.

162
00:10:50.769 --> 00:10:54.945
Taking a complement twice returns the original set.

163
00:10:54.945 --> 00:11:01.209
De Morgan's laws say that the complement of an intersection is the

164
00:11:01.209 --> 00:11:07.473
union of the complements, and the complement of a union is their

165
00:11:07.473 --> 00:11:07.995
intersection.

166
00:11:07.995 --> 00:11:14.258
Translate an arbitrary element's membership into and, or, and not to see

167
00:11:14.258 --> 00:11:16.346
why these laws hold.

168
00:11:16.346 --> 00:11:20.000
Keep the brackets visible when using them.

169
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.800
Now prove that the union of A and B, minus C, equals

170
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:30.633
the union of A minus C and B minus C.

171
00:11:30.633 --> 00:11:34.017
Replace difference by intersection with a complement.

172
00:11:34.017 --> 00:11:39.817
The left side becomes the union of A and B intersected with

173
00:11:39.817 --> 00:11:41.750
the complement of C.

174
00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:44.650
Distribute that intersection across the union.

175
00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:50.450
This gives A intersect the complement of C, union B intersect the

176
00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:51.900
complement of C.

177
00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.283
Convert each piece back to a difference.

178
00:11:55.283 --> 00:12:01.083
Every equality follows from a previously established identity, so the chain proves

179
00:12:01.083 --> 00:12:03.500
equality without checking particular sets.

180
00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:09.782
A point in the plane is specified by an ordered pair of

181
00:12:09.782 --> 00:12:10.829
real numbers.

182
00:12:10.829 --> 00:12:17.112
The first coordinate tells us the horizontal position and the second the

183
00:12:17.112 --> 00:12:18.159
vertical position.

184
00:12:18.159 --> 00:12:24.441
The set of all such pairs is the Cartesian product of the

185
00:12:24.441 --> 00:12:26.535
real numbers with themselves.

186
00:12:26.535 --> 00:12:31.247
Order matters: swapping two unequal coordinates changes the point.

187
00:12:31.247 --> 00:12:37.529
The displayed inequality should be understood in that general sense; if both

188
00:12:37.529 --> 00:12:42.241
coordinates are equal, swapping them gives the same pair.

189
00:12:42.241 --> 00:12:48.000
Ordered pairs are different objects from unordered sets containing two elements.

190
00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.558
A times B is the set of all ordered pairs whose first

191
00:12:53.558 --> 00:12:58.189
coordinate comes from A and second coordinate comes from B.

192
00:12:58.189 --> 00:13:03.747
When A contains one, two, and three, and B contains u and

193
00:13:03.747 --> 00:13:06.063
v, there are six pairs.

194
00:13:06.063 --> 00:13:11.621
Each of the three first coordinates can be combined with either second

195
00:13:11.621 --> 00:13:12.084
coordinate.

196
00:13:12.084 --> 00:13:17.642
A times A has nine pairs, including the three diagonal pairs with

197
00:13:17.642 --> 00:13:18.568
equal coordinates.

198
00:13:18.568 --> 00:13:24.126
For finite sets, the size of the Cartesian product is the product

199
00:13:24.126 --> 00:13:25.516
of their sizes.

200
00:13:25.516 --> 00:13:31.074
The notation A squared means A times A here, not squaring its

201
00:13:31.074 --> 00:13:32.000
individual elements.

202
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.462
Cartesian products are not commutative in general.

203
00:13:35.462 --> 00:13:41.396
With A containing one and B containing u, A times B contains

204
00:13:41.396 --> 00:13:46.835
the pair one, u, while B times A contains u, one.

205
00:13:46.835 --> 00:13:50.297
These pairs differ because their positions differ.

206
00:13:50.297 --> 00:13:56.231
Equality can still occur in special cases, such as A equal to

207
00:13:56.231 --> 00:14:02.165
B, so do not read the slide as saying equality is impossible.

208
00:14:02.165 --> 00:14:06.121
Intersection selects common elements from a shared universe.

209
00:14:06.121 --> 00:14:11.066
A product instead creates pairs, possibly from two different universes.

210
00:14:11.066 --> 00:14:17.000
This change in the kind of object matters in every product proof.

211
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:22.864
Take A to be the closed interval from one to three and

212
00:14:22.864 --> 00:14:26.773
B the closed interval from two to five.

213
00:14:26.773 --> 00:14:31.170
Their Cartesian product is a rectangle in the plane.

214
00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:37.034
Its horizontal coordinates range independently from one to three, while its vertical

215
00:14:37.034 --> 00:14:39.966
coordinates range from two to five.

216
00:14:39.966 --> 00:14:44.364
The boundary is included because both inequalities allow equality.

217
00:14:44.364 --> 00:14:49.250
For example, the pair one, five lies in the product.

218
00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:55.114
The pair two, six does not, because the second coordinate fails the

219
00:14:55.114 --> 00:14:56.580
condition for B.

220
00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.000
Check each coordinate against its own set.

221
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.255
Not every subset of the plane is a rectangle.

222
00:15:04.255 --> 00:15:09.929
The equation x squared plus y squared equals one selects the unit

223
00:15:09.929 --> 00:15:12.293
circle, including only its circumference.

224
00:15:12.293 --> 00:15:17.967
Replacing equality with less than or equal to selects the entire closed

225
00:15:17.967 --> 00:15:18.913
unit disk.

226
00:15:18.913 --> 00:15:24.587
The origin belongs to the disk because zero is at most one,

227
00:15:24.587 --> 00:15:30.261
but it does not belong to the circle because zero is not

228
00:15:30.261 --> 00:15:30.734
one.

229
00:15:30.734 --> 00:15:34.989
The circle is a proper subset of the disk.

230
00:15:34.989 --> 00:15:40.663
Here the coordinates are linked by a joint condition, unlike the independent

231
00:15:40.663 --> 00:15:43.500
interval choices in the rectangle example.

232
00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:49.432
Cartesian products distribute over intersection, union, and difference when the other factor

233
00:15:49.432 --> 00:15:50.420
remains fixed.

234
00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:56.352
For example, A times the union of B and C equals the

235
00:15:56.352 --> 00:16:00.801
union of A times B and A times C.

236
00:16:00.801 --> 00:16:06.733
The same pattern holds with the fixed factor on the right, but

237
00:16:06.733 --> 00:16:10.687
its coordinate position must stay on the right.

238
00:16:10.687 --> 00:16:15.136
These identities follow from statements about the two coordinates.

239
00:16:15.136 --> 00:16:18.597
They do not let us swap factors.

240
00:16:18.597 --> 00:16:24.528
When checking an identity, unpack one ordered pair and preserve the logical

241
00:16:24.528 --> 00:16:27.000
grouping of its membership conditions.

242
00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:31.571
For the forward inclusion, take a pair x, y in A times

243
00:16:31.571 --> 00:16:33.857
the union of B and C.

244
00:16:33.857 --> 00:16:38.429
Then x belongs to A, and y belongs to B or C.

245
00:16:38.429 --> 00:16:43.000
If y belongs to B, the pair lies in A times B;

246
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:47.190
if y belongs to C, it lies in A times C.

247
00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:51.000
Either way it lies in the union of those products.

248
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.571
Conversely, a pair in that union lies in at least one product.

249
00:16:55.571 --> 00:17:00.143
Its first coordinate is in A, and its second is in B

250
00:17:00.143 --> 00:17:03.571
or C, so it lies in the original product.

251
00:17:03.571 --> 00:17:07.000
This supplies the reverse direction left as an exercise.

252
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.500
We now turn to section five point two, functions.

253
00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:15.000
Sets supply the possible inputs and outputs.

254
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.500
A function adds a rule linking them.

255
00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:24.500
Our first task is to check that every permitted input receives exactly

256
00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:25.500
one output.

257
00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:31.500
Later, we ask two extra questions: can distinct inputs share an output,

258
00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:36.000
and does every member of the codomain get reached?

259
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.500
These questions lead to injection and surjection.

260
00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:45.012
A function from A to B assigns every element of A exactly

261
00:17:45.012 --> 00:17:46.849
one element of B.

262
00:17:46.849 --> 00:17:50.983
The words every and exactly one are separate requirements.

263
00:17:50.983 --> 00:17:56.494
No input in A may be left without an output, and no

264
00:17:56.494 --> 00:17:59.250
input may have two competing outputs.

265
00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:02.924
A is the domain and B the codomain.

266
00:18:02.924 --> 00:18:08.436
Different inputs may share an output, and some codomain elements may never

267
00:18:08.436 --> 00:18:09.814
occur as outputs.

268
00:18:09.814 --> 00:18:15.326
A formula without its domain and codomain is therefore not a complete

269
00:18:15.326 --> 00:18:19.000
specification of the function we want to study.

270
00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:24.655
Sine gives one real output for every real input, so it defines

271
00:18:24.655 --> 00:18:29.368
a function from the real numbers to the real numbers.

272
00:18:29.368 --> 00:18:35.023
In particular, zero maps to zero and pi over two maps to

273
00:18:35.023 --> 00:18:35.494
one.

274
00:18:35.494 --> 00:18:41.149
The proposed reciprocal rule from all real numbers to real numbers fails

275
00:18:41.149 --> 00:18:42.092
at zero.

276
00:18:42.092 --> 00:18:46.805
There is no real value for one divided by zero.

277
00:18:46.805 --> 00:18:52.460
We can obtain a valid function by restricting the domain to nonzero

278
00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:53.402
real numbers.

279
00:18:53.402 --> 00:18:59.057
Checking the stated domain prevents an otherwise familiar formula from hiding an

280
00:18:59.057 --> 00:19:00.000
undefined input.

281
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.217
Assigning both the positive and negative square root to a positive input

282
00:19:06.217 --> 00:19:08.289
fails the exactly-one-output condition.

283
00:19:08.289 --> 00:19:14.506
For the input two, the proposed outputs are positive square root of

284
00:19:14.506 --> 00:19:18.133
two and negative square root of two.

285
00:19:18.133 --> 00:19:22.277
Choosing only the nonnegative root gives a function.

286
00:19:22.277 --> 00:19:27.458
By contrast, squaring real inputs is already a valid function.

287
00:19:27.458 --> 00:19:33.675
Two and negative two both map to four, but each input still

288
00:19:33.675 --> 00:19:35.747
has exactly one output.

289
00:19:35.747 --> 00:19:41.964
Sharing outputs concerns injectivity; it does not invalidate the basic definition of

290
00:19:41.964 --> 00:19:43.000
a function.

291
00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:48.103
Write f from A to B to specify a function's domain and

292
00:19:48.103 --> 00:19:48.529
codomain.

293
00:19:48.529 --> 00:19:53.207
If a is in A, f of a is its image.

294
00:19:53.207 --> 00:19:57.034
The input a is a preimage of that output.

295
00:19:57.034 --> 00:20:02.138
Every input has exactly one image, but an element of the codomain

296
00:20:02.138 --> 00:20:05.540
can have no preimages, one preimage, or several.

297
00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:09.368
To find images, evaluate the rule at an input.

298
00:20:09.368 --> 00:20:14.471
To find preimages of a chosen output, solve the equation f of

299
00:20:14.471 --> 00:20:19.575
x equals that output, keeping only solutions that belong to the stated

300
00:20:19.575 --> 00:20:20.000
domain.

301
00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:25.613
For squaring on the real numbers, the image of two is four.

302
00:20:25.613 --> 00:20:31.226
Solving x squared equals four gives two preimages, two and negative two.

303
00:20:31.226 --> 00:20:34.968
Both must be included in the preimage set.

304
00:20:34.968 --> 00:20:40.581
For sine on the real numbers, solving sine x equals zero gives

305
00:20:40.581 --> 00:20:42.919
every integer multiple of pi.

306
00:20:42.919 --> 00:20:48.532
We write this set as k pi, where k is an integer.

307
00:20:48.532 --> 00:20:52.274
Zero, positive multiples, and negative multiples all belong.

308
00:20:52.274 --> 00:20:57.887
These examples show why a preimage set may contain several or infinitely

309
00:20:57.887 --> 00:21:03.500
many inputs even though the function gives only one output per input.

310
00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:08.532
The floor of a real number is the greatest integer less than

311
00:21:08.532 --> 00:21:10.210
or equal to it.

312
00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:15.242
Thus the floor of two point seven four is two, the floor

313
00:21:15.242 --> 00:21:20.274
of negative one point nine is negative two, and the floor of

314
00:21:20.274 --> 00:21:21.532
three is three.

315
00:21:21.532 --> 00:21:26.145
For negative inputs, floor moves toward smaller numbers, not toward zero.

316
00:21:26.145 --> 00:21:31.177
Formally, if the floor of x is n, then n is at

317
00:21:31.177 --> 00:21:35.790
most x and x is strictly less than n plus one.

318
00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:40.823
A function need not be described by a single algebraic formula to

319
00:21:40.823 --> 00:21:42.500
have a precise rule.

320
00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:47.907
The ceiling of a real number is the smallest integer greater than

321
00:21:47.907 --> 00:21:49.709
or equal to it.

322
00:21:49.709 --> 00:21:55.115
The ceiling of two point seven four is three, the ceiling of

323
00:21:55.115 --> 00:22:00.522
negative one point nine is negative one, and the ceiling of three

324
00:22:00.522 --> 00:22:01.423
is three.

325
00:22:01.423 --> 00:22:06.830
If the ceiling is n, then n minus one is strictly less

326
00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:10.434
than x and x is at most n.

327
00:22:10.434 --> 00:22:13.588
Floor and ceiling agree on integer inputs.

328
00:22:13.588 --> 00:22:18.995
On noninteger inputs they select the two adjacent integers that bracket the

329
00:22:18.995 --> 00:22:19.445
number.

330
00:22:19.445 --> 00:22:23.500
Neither operation is ordinary rounding to the nearest integer.

331
00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:29.120
The range is the set of outputs that the function actually produces.

332
00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:34.741
In the arrow diagram, the codomain contains a, b, and c, but

333
00:22:34.741 --> 00:22:38.019
all arrows end at a or b.

334
00:22:38.019 --> 00:22:41.766
The range therefore contains only a and b.

335
00:22:41.766 --> 00:22:45.981
The range is always a subset of the codomain.

336
00:22:45.981 --> 00:22:51.601
Specifying a codomain tells us which outputs are allowed; determining the range

337
00:22:51.601 --> 00:22:53.943
tells us which are reached.

338
00:22:53.943 --> 00:22:59.563
Counting unused codomain elements can immediately show that a finite function is

339
00:22:59.563 --> 00:23:00.500
not onto.

340
00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:06.360
For the real squaring function with real codomain, the range is all

341
00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:07.826
nonnegative real numbers.

342
00:23:07.826 --> 00:23:13.686
Squares cannot be negative, and every nonnegative y is reached by the

343
00:23:13.686 --> 00:23:16.616
real input square root of y.

344
00:23:16.616 --> 00:23:20.523
Both observations are needed for the exact range.

345
00:23:20.523 --> 00:23:26.384
Sine with real domain and codomain has range the closed interval from

346
00:23:26.384 --> 00:23:28.337
negative one to one.

347
00:23:28.337 --> 00:23:33.221
In each example, the codomain is larger than the range.

348
00:23:33.221 --> 00:23:39.081
A range claim should account both for impossible outputs and for why

349
00:23:39.081 --> 00:23:42.500
all the listed outputs really can occur.

350
00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:46.378
We classify functions using two independent properties.

351
00:23:46.378 --> 00:23:53.027
An injection, also called one-to-one, never sends distinct inputs to the same

352
00:23:53.027 --> 00:23:53.581
output.

353
00:23:53.581 --> 00:23:59.676
A surjection, also called onto, reaches every element of its codomain.

354
00:23:59.676 --> 00:24:02.446
A bijection has both properties.

355
00:24:02.446 --> 00:24:09.095
In terms of preimages, injection means at most one per codomain element;

356
00:24:09.095 --> 00:24:14.081
surjection means at least one; bijection means exactly one.

357
00:24:14.081 --> 00:24:20.730
Keep this separate from the original function requirement, which gives exactly one

358
00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:23.500
image to each domain element.

359
00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:29.278
The formal injection condition says that for all x and y in

360
00:24:29.278 --> 00:24:35.056
A, if x differs from y, then f of x differs from

361
00:24:35.056 --> 00:24:36.500
f of y.

362
00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:42.278
The diagram has distinct destinations for its three inputs, so there are

363
00:24:42.278 --> 00:24:43.241
no collisions.

364
00:24:43.241 --> 00:24:47.574
A codomain element can remain unused without violating injection.

365
00:24:47.574 --> 00:24:52.870
Thus injection concerns uniqueness of preimages, not coverage of the codomain.

366
00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:58.648
For an infinite domain we cannot inspect every arrow, so we use

367
00:24:58.648 --> 00:25:02.500
the quantified condition to build an algebraic proof.

368
00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:08.646
The contrapositive gives the usual injection proof: assume f of x equals

369
00:25:08.646 --> 00:25:14.793
f of y for arbitrary domain elements, and derive x equals y.

370
00:25:14.793 --> 00:25:17.866
This is equivalent to the definition.

371
00:25:17.866 --> 00:25:21.451
To disprove injection, negate the universal implication.

372
00:25:21.451 --> 00:25:26.061
Find two distinct domain elements with the same image.

373
00:25:26.061 --> 00:25:32.207
You must verify both that the inputs differ and that their outputs

374
00:25:32.207 --> 00:25:32.720
agree.

375
00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:38.866
Showing that one output has a preimage does not address injection, because

376
00:25:38.866 --> 00:25:44.500
the question is whether that output could have two different preimages.

377
00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:50.214
Consider f of x equals five x plus three from the real

378
00:25:50.214 --> 00:25:52.595
numbers to the real numbers.

379
00:25:52.595 --> 00:25:58.310
Take arbitrary real x and y, and suppose their images are equal.

380
00:25:58.310 --> 00:26:03.071
Then five x plus three equals five y plus three.

381
00:26:03.071 --> 00:26:08.786
Subtracting three yields five x equals five y, and dividing by the

382
00:26:08.786 --> 00:26:12.119
nonzero number five gives x equals y.

383
00:26:12.119 --> 00:26:14.500
Therefore the function is injective.

384
00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:17.357
The proof does not assume injectivity.

385
00:26:17.357 --> 00:26:23.071
It starts with equal outputs and shows that no distinct inputs could

386
00:26:23.071 --> 00:26:24.500
have produced them.

387
00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:30.579
For f of x equals x squared plus one on all real

388
00:26:30.579 --> 00:26:34.632
numbers, choose the inputs two and negative two.

389
00:26:34.632 --> 00:26:38.684
They are distinct, but both outputs equal five.

390
00:26:38.684 --> 00:26:41.217
This single collision disproves injection.

391
00:26:41.217 --> 00:26:43.750
Notice how the domain matters.

392
00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:49.829
If only positive real inputs were allowed, this particular pair would be

393
00:26:49.829 --> 00:26:54.388
unavailable, and the function would in fact be injective.

394
00:26:54.388 --> 00:27:00.467
Whenever you use a counterexample, check membership in the actual domain before

395
00:27:00.467 --> 00:27:03.000
treating the pair as decisive.

396
00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:08.392
A function from A to B is surjective when every y in

397
00:27:08.392 --> 00:27:11.538
B has some preimage x in A.

398
00:27:11.538 --> 00:27:16.930
The order of quantifiers matters: first choose an arbitrary target y, then

399
00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:20.525
find an input that may depend on y.

400
00:27:20.525 --> 00:27:25.918
The diagram reaches all three codomain elements, even though two inputs share

401
00:27:25.918 --> 00:27:27.266
one of them.

402
00:27:27.266 --> 00:27:30.411
Such sharing is allowed in a surjection.

403
00:27:30.411 --> 00:27:35.804
The proof obligation is complete coverage of B, not uniqueness of the

404
00:27:35.804 --> 00:27:38.500
input used to reach each target.

405
00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:43.380
Surjection is equivalent to saying the range equals the codomain.

406
00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:49.235
To prove it, show every codomain element is produced; the reverse containment

407
00:27:49.235 --> 00:27:53.627
already follows from being a function into that codomain.

408
00:27:53.627 --> 00:27:59.482
To disprove it, find one y in B that is unequal to

409
00:27:59.482 --> 00:28:03.386
f of x for every x in A.

410
00:28:03.386 --> 00:28:06.313
The witness is an unreachable output.

411
00:28:06.313 --> 00:28:12.169
This differs from disproving injection, where the witness consists of two inputs

412
00:28:12.169 --> 00:28:13.633
with equal outputs.

413
00:28:13.633 --> 00:28:19.000
Choosing the correct type of witness makes the proof much clearer.

414
00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:24.461
Again take f of x equals five x plus three from real

415
00:28:24.461 --> 00:28:26.281
numbers to real numbers.

416
00:28:26.281 --> 00:28:29.466
Let y be an arbitrary real target.

417
00:28:29.466 --> 00:28:34.927
Solving y equals five x plus three suggests x equals y minus

418
00:28:34.927 --> 00:28:37.202
three, all divided by five.

419
00:28:37.202 --> 00:28:41.753
This candidate is real, so it belongs to the domain.

420
00:28:41.753 --> 00:28:44.938
Substituting it into f gives y exactly.

421
00:28:44.938 --> 00:28:50.399
We have constructed a valid preimage for every real target, so the

422
00:28:50.399 --> 00:28:51.764
function is onto.

423
00:28:51.764 --> 00:28:57.225
Solving for a candidate is the discovery step; verifying its domain and

424
00:28:57.225 --> 00:28:59.500
its image completes the proof.

425
00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:05.221
For x squared plus one with real domain and real codomain, negative

426
00:29:05.221 --> 00:29:07.605
two is an unreachable target.

427
00:29:07.605 --> 00:29:13.326
If some real x mapped to negative two, then x squared plus

428
00:29:13.326 --> 00:29:19.047
one would equal negative two, giving x squared equal to negative three.

429
00:29:19.047 --> 00:29:24.291
A real square cannot be negative, so no such x exists.

430
00:29:24.291 --> 00:29:29.058
This proves the function is not onto the real numbers.

431
00:29:29.058 --> 00:29:33.826
Its actual range is all real numbers at least one.

432
00:29:33.826 --> 00:29:39.547
An unreachable witness must lie inside the specified codomain, as negative two

433
00:29:39.547 --> 00:29:40.500
does here.

434
00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:44.105
A bijection is both injective and surjective.

435
00:29:44.105 --> 00:29:50.285
We proved both properties for five x plus three on the real

436
00:29:50.285 --> 00:29:50.800
numbers.

437
00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:56.980
The second example shows the role of the domain and codomain: x

438
00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:03.160
squared plus one maps positive real inputs bijectively onto real outputs greater

439
00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:04.190
than one.

440
00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:08.310
Equal outputs force equal positive inputs, proving injection.

441
00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:14.490
For any target y greater than one, the positive square root of

442
00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:19.125
y minus one is a valid preimage, proving surjection.

443
00:30:19.125 --> 00:30:25.305
As you review, turn each set or function definition into its quantified

444
00:30:25.305 --> 00:30:31.485
proof obligation, then choose either an arbitrary element argument or the correct

445
00:30:31.485 --> 00:30:32.000
counterexample.
